Answer: 
 $$f(x) =\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{-1+\sqrt{1+8 x}}$$ 

 The argument of the square root is 0, hence $f$ has no derivative in 0. That is why you get non integer powers. 
 First, get the equivalent: 
 $$\sqrt{1+8 x}-1 \sim 4x\\
f(x) = \frac 12\sqrt{-1+\sqrt{1+8 x}}\sim \frac 12\sqrt{4x} = \sqrt{x}
$$
and then:
$$
f(x) -\sqrt{x} 
= \frac 12\sqrt{-1+\sqrt{1+8 x}} - \frac 12\sqrt{4x}
\\= \frac 12 \frac{-1+\sqrt{1+8 x} +4x}
{\sqrt{-1+\sqrt{1+8 x}} - \sqrt{4x}}
\sim 
\frac 12 \frac{-\frac 18 (8x)^2}{4\sqrt{x}}=-x\sqrt{x}
$$ 
