Answer: The probability of  not  getting a number $a$ on a six-sided dice is $\mathrm{Pr}(a)=\dfrac{5}{6}$. The probability of getting $a$ in the first roll is $1-\mathrm{Pr}(a)$. However, see that whether or not you get $a$ in the first roll does not affect the second roll, i.e., rolling a dice is an event independent of the previous roll(s). Thus, the probability of getting $a$ in the $n$th roll is equivalent to getting $a$ in the first roll, i.e., $1-\mathrm{Pr}(a)$. (However, if you want the probability of getting $a$ in all $n$ rolls, the probability is $1-(\mathrm{Pr}(a))^n$.) 
